#heading(numbering: none, outlined: false)[Abstract]

A contended lock decides whose critical section runs next. Patel et al. showed that granting turns in FIFO order is not enough: threads with longer critical sections take more of the lock and subvert the CPU scheduler. Usage-fair locks address this directly. u-SCL measures how long each thread holds the lock, grants lock slices, and bans threads that overuse it; CFL reorders the wait queue by virtual lock hold time, in the manner of CFS. Both restore a fair allocation in real systems. Both, however, keep the thread being served as the thread that runs the critical section, so every fair hand-off moves the protected data to another core, and both win back locality by giving up some fairness: u-SCL lets a slice owner reacquire the lock for milliseconds, and CFL groups waiters by socket. Each also needs a rule for a thread that is owed service but is not waiting when the lock decides, for example because it is still waking up: u-SCL holds the lock idle for its slice owner, while CFL gives the turn to whoever is waiting. We call these two costs _migration_ and _absence_. Delegation locks remove migration, because a combiner runs every request where the data already is; absence remains, and becomes an explicit waiting policy. We present FC-PQ, a flat-combining lock whose combiner serves the pending request with the least accumulated critical-section time, charged to the requesting thread. Inside redb's single-writer path at a fixed 3.0 GHz clock, FC-PQ raises service-time Jain fairness between 1-record and 64-record writers from 0.85--0.86 under MCS to 0.94--0.995, runs 1.17--1.34× as many transactions per second, and incurs 10--18 cross-core cache transfers per transaction where MCS incurs 93--126. It ties u-SCL, because redb's writers resubmit at once and are never absent. Making FC-PQ's waiters block exposes absence directly: with identical transactions, blocking matches u-SCL's CPU time at spinning throughput, but with mixed sizes short-transaction clients wake too late and fairness falls as low as 0.65.
